{% set local_problem %}
<p>Athens' older neighborhoods face drainage challenges that surface solutions can't fully address. Many homes in established areas near <a href="/recent-projects/">Athens State University, around downtown, and throughout central Limestone County</a> were built with drainage systems designed for a different era. Those systems have been patched, modified, and worked around for decades — but never truly upgraded.</p>

<p>The result: downspouts dumping water too close to foundations, surface extensions that look ugly and get damaged by mowers, splash blocks that erode over time, persistent moisture problems that never quite go away. Add Limestone County's clay soil that doesn't absorb water quickly, and foundations sit in saturated soil for days after every heavy rain.</p>

<p>Surface solutions only move water a few feet from your foundation. On clay, that's not far enough — water pools and slowly migrates back toward your home. Underground drains solve this permanently by routing water 20, 30, or even 50+ feet away through sealed pipes. The water never touches foundation soil.</p>

<p>Athens' rolling topography is actually an advantage here. Unlike flat subdivisions where underground drains have to work against gravity, Athens properties often have natural slopes moving water away efficiently. We design systems that use your terrain, routing water downhill to natural discharge points.</p>

<p><strong>The investment perspective:</strong> Underground drains for a typical Athens home run $1,200-$2,400. Foundation repair runs $10,000-30,000. We've seen homeowners spend years dealing with moisture problems, trying cheaper fixes, before finally installing underground drains and solving the issue. Should've done it first.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>Underground drains are buried pipes connecting to your downspouts and carrying water through a sealed, sloped channel to a discharge point far from your home. They work continuously and automatically — every time it rains, water routes away before it can threaten your foundation.</p>

<p><strong>What we install:</strong> 4-inch Schedule 40 PVC pipe with smooth interior walls. Proper slope (1-2% grade) ensuring water flows continuously. Pop-up emitters or daylight exits at discharge points. Clean-out access for future maintenance.</p>

<p><strong>What we don't install:</strong> Corrugated flex pipe. This is critical. That black corrugated drainage pipe from Home Depot seems like a cheap alternative, but the corrugations trap sediment and debris. Roots grow into the ridges. Within 3-5 years, most corrugated pipe is partially or completely clogged.</p>

<p>We dig up failed corrugated systems in Athens constantly — homeowners thought they solved the problem, but created a new one. Smooth PVC costs more upfront but actually works for decades.</p>

<p><strong>The distinction:</strong> Underground gutter drains handle roof runoff. French drains handle groundwater already in soil. Different systems for different problems. Some Athens properties need both. We assess what's actually going on with your water and recommend accordingly.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set why_us %}
<p>We've installed underground drainage throughout <a href="/recent-projects/">Athens and Limestone County</a> for over 20 years. We understand the soil conditions, the topography variations across neighborhoods, and the specific drainage patterns affecting Athens homes.</p>

<p>Our systems are designed to last decades with minimal maintenance. Quality materials, proper installation techniques, thoughtful design. When you work with Blue River Gutters, you're getting local expertise backed by 20+ years in this area.</p>

<p><strong>Warning from experience:</strong> Every spring we dig up corrugated pipe that failed. Homeowners installed it themselves or hired a landscaper who didn't know better. The pipe is clogged, roots have invaded, water isn't flowing. They end up paying twice — once for the failed system, once for us to replace it with smooth PVC. Do it right the first time.</p>

<p><strong>Our opinion:</strong> If you're spending money on underground drainage, spend the extra 20-30% for smooth PVC. The corrugated stuff isn't cheaper when you account for the fact that it fails and needs replacement. This is one place where "saving money" costs more in the long run.</p>
{% endset %}

